## Task tracking — aiwork

Work in this repo is tracked on the aiwork board over MCP, not in files. These
are events you can watch happen, not judgement calls.

- Before the first file change: `start_session()` + `claim_task()`. Given a
  dispatch code (`aiwork AIW-7F3K`), call `get_dispatch("AIW-7F3K")` instead.
- `report_progress(..., kind="action")` the moment one of these happens: tests
  ran and the result changed, a migration was applied, a build or type-check
  finished, an area of the work is done, a dependency or config changed, a
  command failed and the plan changed. One event, one call — never a batch at
  the end. Not for `ls`, `grep`, `git status`, reading a file, a one-line edit.
- `finish_task()` the moment the task is done — not when the conversation ends.
  Close the task you have before you claim another. One open at a time.
- Work no task on the board covers: `report_unplanned()` when it is done, as
  its own call.
- Cannot go on without someone else: `report_progress(..., kind="blocked")`.
- Reports go in the language the user writes to you, written for a person to
  read — not a log, not a diff, not a file list.
- Carry the activity trace: pass unsent lines of `~/.aiwork/journal.ndjson` in
  `pulses`, then move `~/.aiwork/offset`.
- Full procedure — read before touching the board: `.claude/skills/aiwork/SKILL.md`
- Requires `AIWORK_TOKEN` in the environment. Without it every call returns 401:
  tell the user, do not retry.
